a1319.en:
The Council of Ministers met in Ouagadougou on Wednesday, 27 November 2024, in ordinary session, from 09:00 a.m. to 12:50 p.m., under the chairmanship of His Excellency Captain Ibrahim TRAORE, President of Faso, President of the Council of Ministers. It deliberated on the matters on its agenda, heard oral communications, made appointments, and authorised missions abroad.

- DELIBERATIONS

I.1. UNDER THE PRESIDENCY OF FASO

The Council adopted a report on the 2024 specific projects of the National Bureau of Major Projects of Burkina (BN-GPB).

As part of the operationalisation of the Action Plan for Stabilisation and Development (PA-SD), one of the major actions is the creation of several presidential initiatives housed within the National Bureau of Major Projects of Burkina (BN-GPB). These are:
- the Presidential Initiative for Agricultural Production and Food Self-Sufficiency (IPP3A);
- the Presidential Initiative for Quality Education (IPEQ);
- the Presidential Initiative for Community Development (IPDC);
- the Presidential Initiative for Health for All (IPS).

The objective of this report is the construction of infrastructure and the acquisition of goods within reduced timeframes for the benefit of the relevant sectoral ministries.

Specifically, these involve:
- the carrying out of architectural and technical studies, the construction and the monitoring/supervision of 25 communal medical centres, as well as the acquisition of specific equipment for the Yalgado OUEDRAOGO University Hospital Centre and the Sourô SANON University Hospital Centre under the Presidential Initiative for Health for All (IPS), for an amount of 21,494,211,984 CFA francs including tax;
- the construction and monitoring/supervision of 06 lecture halls under the Presidential Initiative for Quality Education (IPEQ), for an amount of 36,415,000,000 CFA francs including tax;
- the acquisition of agricultural equipment under the Presidential Initiative for Agricultural Production and Food Self-Sufficiency (IPP3A), for an amount of 2,915,317,250 CFA francs excluding VAT.

The total cumulative amount of these specific projects comes to sixty billion eight hundred twenty-four million five hundred twenty-nine thousand two hundred thirty-four (60,824,529,234) CFA francs.

The financing is provided by the State budget.

I.2. UNDER THE MINISTRY OF ECONOMY AND FINANCE

The Council adopted two (02) decrees.

The first decree authorises the collection of revenue relating to certain services of the General Secretariat of the Government and of the Council of Ministers.

This decree is a revision of decree no. 2010-056/PRES/PM/MEF of 19 February 2010 authorising the collection of revenue relating to certain services of the printing house of the Official Journal.

The major innovations of the decree concern the inclusion of the following new services:
- online sale of the Official Journal in digital format;
- online insertion of receipts in the Official Journal;
- insertion of orders and decisions issued for the benefit of legal or natural persons engaged in a for-profit activity or for a Non-Governmental Organisation;
- publication of reports and other documents of ministries and institutions;
- publication of compilations of legal texts;
- publication in the Official Journal of Burkina Faso of conventions or contracts;
- issuance of copies of archives in the form of thematic kits.

The adoption of this decree allows the General Secretariat of the Government and of the Council of Ministers to improve its contribution to the State budget.

The second decree declares to be of urgent public utility the power lines, the substations and the power plants, the high-voltage power line construction projects and their corridors, as well as the substations and power plants of the National Electricity Company of Burkina (SONABEL).

As part of the implementation of emergency projects aimed at addressing the electricity production deficit and energy dependence, the Ministry of Energy, Mines and Quarries, through SONABEL, plans to carry out new energy infrastructure construction projects. These consist of twenty-three (23) high-voltage power line construction projects, five (05) electrical substation construction projects and eight (08) power plant construction projects.

These projects cannot be carried out without clearing the rights-of-way of the sites concerned. Moreover, decree no. 2023-0299/PRES-TRANS/PM/MEFP/MEEA/MID/MTMUSR of 24 March 2023, amending and extending the deadline of decree no. 2019-1129/PM/MINEFID/MATDCS/ME/MTMUSR of 16 November 2019, had declared power lines, high-voltage power line construction projects and their corridors to be of urgent public utility. However, that decree expired on 15 November 2024, while some new projects have been initiated and others are still ongoing and require secure land tenure.

The adoption of this decree makes it possible to declare the rights-of-way of the sites of these projects of the National Electricity Company of Burkina to be of urgent public utility.

I.3. UNDER THE MINISTRY OF JUSTICE AND HUMAN RIGHTS, IN CHARGE OF RELATIONS WITH INSTITUTIONS

The Council examined a draft law amending law no. 020/97/II/AN of 1 August 1997 on the description and meaning of the Coat of Arms of Burkina Faso.

This amendment aims to take into account our country's new motto as resulting from Constitutional law no. 033-2024/ALT of 29 October 2024. It will bring the coat of arms described in law no. 020/97/II/AN of 1 August 1997 on the description and meaning of the coat of arms of Burkina Faso into conformity by retaining "The Homeland or Death, We Shall Overcome" as our country's motto.

The Council agreed to the transmission of the said draft law to the Transitional Legislative Assembly.

I.4. UNDER THE MINISTRY OF INDUSTRY, TRADE AND CRAFTS

The Council adopted a decree approving the statutes of the Rail Manufacturing and Laying Company "FASO RAILS".

The adoption of this decree allows the Rail Manufacturing and Laying Company "FASO RAILS" to have regulatory texts enabling it to carry out its missions, in accordance with law no. 025/99/AN of 16 November 1999 on the general regulation of companies with public capital.

I.5. UNDER THE MINISTRY OF ENERGY, MINES AND QUARRIES

The Council adopted two (02) decrees.

The first decree sets mining taxes and royalties.

This decree is aimed at the application of law no. 036-2024/ALT of 18 July 2024 on the Mining Code of Burkina Faso.

The innovations of this decree concern, among other things:
- the determination of fixed duties and surface-area taxes according to the category of substances applied for rather than by individual mineral substance;
- the reduction of the maximum period for payment of proportional royalties for mining substances from 02 months to 21 days;
- the payment of surface-area taxes no later than 31 March of the current year;
- the upward revision of fixed duties and surface-area taxes;
- the creation of new fixed duties and royalties;
- the setting of proportional royalties levied on gold exports according to movements in prices.

The adoption of this decree allows our country to have new regulatory texts on mining taxes and royalties in order to improve State revenue linked to the mining sector.

The second decree sets the conditions and procedures for the transfer of the State's mining assets.

Under the 2015 Mining Code, the mining administration recorded mining assets consisting essentially of property, mining data, equipment and mining titles and/or authorisations that had been withdrawn, relinquished or had expired. With the revision of the former mining code, emphasis was placed on the transfer of the State's mining assets through the adoption of law no. 036-2024/ALT of 18 July 2024 on the Mining Code of Burkina Faso.

This decree is aimed at the application of the new law on the Mining Code of Burkina Faso and includes innovations which are, among others:
- the setting of specific conditions and procedures for the transfer of mining assets;
- optimisation in the transfer of mining assets through a multidisciplinary evaluation body;
- better revaluation of the State's mining assets;
- the creation of additional revenue for the State budget.

The adoption of this decree allows our country to have regulatory texts specific to the transfer of mining assets.

- ORAL COMMUNICATIONS

II.1. The Minister of Economy and Finance, serving as acting Minister of Foreign Affairs, Regional Cooperation and Burkinabè Abroad, reported to the Council on Burkina Faso's participation in the ministerial conference of the Russia-Africa Partnership Forum, held from 09 to 10 November 2024 in Sochi, Russia.

This ministerial conference of the Russia-Africa Partnership Forum was attended by nearly fifty African countries. It was marked by exchanges between the Minister of Foreign Affairs of the Russian Federation and the heads of delegation, as well as by plenary sessions.

At this conference, the Minister of Foreign Affairs of the Russian Federation advocated for a multipolar world order free of neocolonialism, in which Africa must play a leading role commensurate with its potential.

At the end of the proceedings, the participants adopted a joint declaration on strengthening the various areas of cooperation. These main areas are the economy, humanitarian affairs, investment, security, the fight against terrorism, culture and education. This declaration is accompanied by a roadmap for the period 2024-2026.

II.2. The Minister of Humanitarian Action and National Solidarity presented to the Council an assessment of the multisectoral needs of Internally Displaced Persons (IDPs) who have returned to their localities of origin in five (05) regions of Burkina Faso.

This assessment, which concerns IDPs in the regions of Boucle du Mouhoun, Cascades, Hauts-Bassins, Nord and Sud-Ouest, aims to target and adapt multisectoral actions, particularly with regard to the rehabilitation of infrastructure, access to basic social services and the restoration of livelihoods.

In order to establish the needs involved, a survey was carried out among 469,898 returned Internally Displaced Persons from 59,972 households in these five (05) regions.

The multisectoral needs assessment made it possible to identify the actions to be implemented for a sustainable reintegration of the returnees into their localities of origin in these targeted regions.

